# Tea Bot — core invariants

Production WhatsApp conversation platform: Node/Express backend, React admin, SQL Server, Twilio webhooks, realtime conversation UI.

## Architecture

- Keep webhook ingress, idempotency (`provider_webhook_events`, acquire/lease), flow engine, conversation lifecycle, and admin API boundaries.
- Controllers stay thin; business rules live in services; SQL in repositories.
- Preserve published flows in SQL; do not silently change flow semantics without migration/import discipline.

## Security and webhooks

- Twilio signature verification is mandatory in production paths; do not bypass for convenience.
- Enforce role checks on the backend for takeover, close, and return-to-bot (`admin` and `conversations_only` per product rules).
- Never commit secrets; use `.env.example` patterns only.

## Concurrency and integrity

- Treat inbound idempotency, processing leases, fencing stale workers, and exclusive agent identity as integrity guarantees.
- Conversation mutations must respect optimistic concurrency (`status_version`) and audit expectations.
- Integration tests against SQL Server must avoid cross-file DB contention; prefer isolated DB helpers when the repo provides them.

## Frontend

- Colocate feature code under `frontend/src/features/`.
- Preserve live update and client request id patterns for takeover UX.
- Run `typecheck` and tests when touching conversation UI or API client types.

## Deploy

- Respect Docker Compose and documented migration order.
- Feature flags (`MANUAL_TAKEOVER_ENABLED`, Twilio modes) change behavior — document toggles when editing config.
